{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references` has one MUST: each link from a bundled skill to a file of specialized material must say when to read that file. The MAY (putting such material in linked files) imposes nothing to check. Each skill upholds the MUST on its own, so it belongs to the per-site reviews and needs no obligation-specific code or test. The distributed requirement therefore leaves no obligation uncovered.\n\n**Site query `skills`.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills`. It matched five files, one per bundled skill:\n- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-evidence/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`\n- `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`\n\n`assets/skills` contains exactly these five directories. A search for `^name:` front matter across the repo found no other skill. The only other hits were GitHub workflow YAML, which is not a skill. The query selects the `references/*.md` files in these skills only if they have front matter, and none do, so they are not selected. I found no missed place and no place selected outside the requirement's scope.\n\n**Query snippets.** The match snippet has front matter followed by a heading, so it shows what the query selects. The no_match snippet is the same heading without front matter, which is the nearby non-skill markdown the query must not select. This covers the `minus_metadata` condition adequately. There are no violation queries.\n\n**Supporting test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]` carries a {test} reference. It asserts that each listed link sentence, which states when to read the file, appears in the corresponding skill. It also scans every skill file for `references/*.md` pointers and requires each pointer to fall inside one of the listed guidance sentences. Finally, it requires every reference file of every skill to have at least one checked link. An unguided link, or a new reference file with no checked sentence, would fail the test. This reinforces the per-site obligation, and I found no gap.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions.references",
        "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
